         "Cuscuta is an obligate parasite dependent on successful host colonization before embryonic seed reserves are exhausted, transitioning from brief ground germination to an entirely aerial parasitic lifestyle.",
         "1. Embryonic reserve limit: the microscopic Cuscuta seed contains only a tiny starch store.\n2. Ground germination: produces a rudimentary thread-like anchor root and a yellow, leafless, questing stem tip.\n3. Chemotropic host seeking: the revolving stem tip detects volatile airborne chemical odors emitted by nearby host plants (like tomato or alfalfa).\n4. Colonization window: if it touches a suitable host stem within 5-10 days, it twines tightly and forms penetrating haustoria.\n5. Root severance: once haustoria establish vascular connection with host xylem and phloem, the subterranean root rots and detaches.\n6. Starvation fate: if no host is contacted before seed reserves run out, the seedling cannot photosynthesize and dies.\n7. Conclude: high-stakes parasitic developmental transition from soil germination to aerial parasitism.",
